This page lists everything the `ogygia` package exposes, grouped by import path, along with every option of the `ogygia()` Vite plugin. Where a symbol has its own page, the entry links to it. The other packages document their own entry points in their sections; the [table at the end](#plugin-packages) links to each.

## `ogygia`

This is the runtime surface. You can import the namespace (`import * as ogygia from 'ogygia'`) or named exports.

| Export          | Kind       | What it is                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |
| --------------- | ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `Boundary`      | component  | Error boundary for island subtrees.                                                                                                                                                                                              |
| `Region`        | component  | Renders a [held region](/docs/regions/held-regions): `<Region of={x} />`.                                                                                                                                                        |
| `region`        | function   | Mints a held region: `region(Component, props)`.                                                                                                                                                                                 |
| `isRegion`      | function   | Type guard for a held region value.                                                                                                                                                                                              |
| `transport`     | hook value | The Kit `transport` entry that lets a held region cross the wire.                                                                                                                                                                |
| `wire`          | macro      | Declare the codec of a transportable class with `static wire = import.meta.og.wire({ encode, decode })`. It is a compile-time macro, not an importable symbol. See [shared state](/docs/data-state/state-context#sharing-state). |
| `script`        | function   | An inline `<script>` string: a self-contained function (`run`), or JSON / JSON-LD / an import map / speculation rules, with typed attributes. See [Pre-hydration scripts](#ogygia-pre-hydration-scripts).                        |
| `preload`       | function   | Warm the HTML of a deferred/live region now, before its binder wakes: `preload(region(…))`. See [held regions](/docs/regions/held-regions).                                                                                      |
| `preference`    | function   | A persisted, site-wide client choice that flips a CSS state with no shipped JS beyond one pre-paint script: the theme / code-language / tabs primitive.                                                                          |
| `createContext` | function   | Typed sugar over a string context key: `createContext('key')`, callable for a `<Provide>` entry, `.get()` to read. See [state & context](/docs/data-state/state-context).                                                        |
| `Provide`       | component  | Provide context values to islands below it across the DOM: `<Provide values={[ctx(v)]}>`.                                                                                                                                        |
| `setContext`    | function   | Drop-in for the `setContext` of Svelte, which also bridges string-keyed values to child islands (the flat page root).                                                                                                            |
| `hydratedBy`    | function   | Which schedule woke this hydration root (for example `'interaction'`). Call it during setup, like `getContext`.                                                                                                                  |

Types: `RegionValue`, `AwaitableRegion`, `InlineRegion`, `DualRegion`, `DeferredRegion`, `TransportCodec`.

### Import attributes

These are written on a component import rather than called:

| Attribute         | Makes                                                                        | Values                                                                       |
| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `with { wake }`   | the schedule: when an island's JS runs (or when a `live` region revalidates) | `'load'` · `'idle'` · `'visible'` · `'interaction'` · `'(media)'` · `'none'` |
| `with { fetch }`  | the schedule of a `render: 'deferred'` hole: when its HTML arrives           | `'load'` (default) · `'idle'` · `'visible'` · `'interaction'` · `'(media)'`  |
| `with { render }` | the delivery mode                                                            | `'static'` (default) · `'deferred'` (server island) · `'live'` (revalidates) |
| `with { region }` | a held, server-chosen region marker                                          | `'raw'`                                                                      |
| `with { keep }`   | keep the live island across SPA navigation                                   | a name                                                                       |
| `with { preset }` | a named preset from plugin config                                            | any preset name                                                              |

`render` sets the mode and `wake` the schedule. A `render: 'deferred'` hole takes `fetch` for its schedule instead of `wake`, and the old pairing of `render: 'deferred'` with `wake` is deprecated (it still works, with a build warning). [Regions](/docs/regions/regions) explains the concept.

### Pre-hydration scripts

A csr=false page has no client until its islands wake, so anything that must run **before the first paint** has to be a plain inline `<script>` in the HTML. That includes setting the theme (to avoid a dark-mode flash), starting a deferred font, or reading a flag early. Writing that string by hand is awkward, because a literal `</script>` inside a Svelte component closes the component's own script, which is why people resort to tricks like `String.fromCharCode(60)`.

`script()` removes that problem. It takes ONE plain object containing exactly one payload key alongside the tag's attributes. A bare function followed by arguments is the shortcut for a plain classic script, so `script(fn, a, b)` means `{ run: fn, args: [a, b] }`. What you get back is a `<script>…</script>` string, and since it is only a string, you `{@html}` it wherever you want the tag, usually in `<svelte:head>`.

```svelte
<script>
  import * as ogygia from 'ogygia';
  import fontUrl from './mono.css?url';
</script>

<svelte:head>
  <!-- No-flash theme, runs before paint (the shortcut) -->
  {@html ogygia.script(() => {
    try {
      const t = localStorage.getItem('theme');
      if (t === 'light' || t === 'dark') document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', t);
    } catch {}
  })}

  <!-- Deferred font: the hashed URL is closed-over data, so pass it in `args` -->
  {@html ogygia.script({
    run: (href) => {
      addEventListener('load', () => {
        const l = document.createElement('link');
        l.rel = 'stylesheet';
        l.href = href;
        document.head.appendChild(l);
      }, { once: true });
    },
    args: [fontUrl],
    type: 'module'
  })}

  <!-- Data: structured data for search engines -->
  {@html ogygia.script({ ld: { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Article', headline } })}
</svelte:head>
```

| Payload key      | Tag                                    | What it is                                                |
| ---------------- | -------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| `run` (+ `args`) | `<script>` or `<script type="module">` | A self-contained function, called with `args`.            |
| `json`           | `type="application/json"`              | Data for code on the page (`JSON.parse(el.textContent)`). |
| `ld`             | `type="application/ld+json"`           | Structured data for search engines.                       |
| `importmap`      | `type="importmap"`                     | Bare specifiers for the module scripts of the page.       |
| `speculation`    | `type="speculationrules"`              | The pages the browser may prefetch or prerender.          |

Attributes go in the same object. `id`, `nonce` and any `data-*` attribute work on every kind of script. `true` writes a bare attribute, while `false`, `null` and `undefined` leave it out, and all values are escaped. Because the object can be spread, a preset is a plain object: `script({ ...base, run })`.

A `run` script is CLASSIC by default, which means it runs where it stands, before the parser continues, so in `<head>` it runs before the first paint. `type: 'module'` gives it its own scope and `import()`, and it then runs after the document is parsed. A module also accepts `async: true` (run as soon as it is ready) and `imports`, the modules it needs by name and URL (`imports: { carousel: url }`). These are written as static `import * as` lines before the call, and `run` receives them as ONE object first, followed by its `args`: `run: ({ carousel }, n) => …`. Declare a module there and never `import()` it inside `run`, because a bundler rewrites code that it compiles (Vite's SSR transform turns `import()` into a helper the browser does not have) but cannot touch a string, and `script()` throws when it detects such a rewrite. The first paint does not wait for a module, so code that must apply before paint should stay a classic script. `async` and `defer` have no effect on an inline classic script, so TypeScript rejects them there, and `blocking="render"` is not offered because it does not hold the paint for an inline module either. TypeScript also rejects `args` that don't match the parameters of `run`, an argument that JSON would alter (a `Date`, a `Map`, a function), and a data script that contains code or sets its own `type`.

The function is inlined through `Function.prototype.toString`, so it must be **self-contained**: it can use browser globals only, with no imports and no closed-over variables, because those do not exist at runtime. An arrow function, a `function`, or a method on the object (`{ run() { … } }`) all work. Values you would otherwise close over go in `args`, where they are JSON-serialized and passed to the function as parameters. Every `<` in an argument or a data payload is escaped, and in the code every `</script` and every `<!--` is escaped (a `<!--` followed by `<script` would make the page swallow everything after the tag), so nothing can break out of the tag.

## `ogygia/vite`

```ts
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';
```

`ogygia(options)` is the Vite plugin, and it runs before `sveltekit()`. Every option is optional. The top level of the options configures the region system itself: its defaults (`visible`), its named presets (`presets`), and how long its holes' URLs live (`ttl`, `maxPageAge`). The client router is configured alongside them.

```ts
ogygia({
  visible: { margin: '120px' },
  presets: {
    demo: { wake: 'visible', margin: '200px' },
    frozenLive: { render: 'live', wake: 'load' }
  }
})
```

Every feature that lives in its own package (content, flags, postrender, federation, the server router, devtools) is a separate Vite plugin, listed beside `ogygia()` in the `plugins` array of your Vite config, where `ogygia()` finds it: `plugins: [ogygia(), flags(), postrender({ ttl: 600 }), sveltekit()]`. Each plugin keeps its own settings, so a region preset can never hold content config, or the reverse. See [Packages](/docs/start/packages).

The old `regions: { … }` wrapper is a build error that names the new form; its keys moved unchanged to the top level.

### `visible`

Sets the default `rootMargin` for `visible` islands and server islands, so that they start loading slightly before they enter the viewport.

```ts
ogygia({ visible: { margin: '200px' } })
```

There is no preload setting. The page carries each island's chunk list as data, and the runtime preloads an island's whole graph when it wakes, after the page has painted (see [how the bytes arrive](/docs/regions/timing-nesting)). The old `preload` key is a build error.

### `router`

The SPA router is on by default for the whole app (see [SPA router](/docs/app/spa-router)). This option is the only place router config lives; there is no `<Router/>` component.

```ts
ogygia()                                       // router on, view transitions on, form continuity on
ogygia({ router: { viewTransitions: false } }) // SPA nav, but no view transitions
ogygia({ router: { forms: false } })           // SPA nav, but form fields don't survive navigation
ogygia({ router: false })                      // opt out of the SPA router entirely
```

`router: false` tree-shakes the router out of the generated runtime, so a load-only app never ships it. It also removes [form continuity](/docs/data-state/state-context#continuity-surviving-navigation), since there is then no SPA navigation for form fields to survive. A single page can opt out of view transitions with `<meta name="ogygia-router" content="plain">` in its head, and the page's tag takes precedence over the global default.

### `barrels()`

The barrel rewrite is a plugin, `barrels()` from `@ogygia/barrels`, listed beside `ogygia()`. The old `ogygia({ barrels })` option is a build error that names it, and the standalone `debarrel()` from `ogygia/vite` is gone.

```ts
import { barrels } from '@ogygia/barrels';
export default defineConfig({ plugins: [ogygia(), barrels(), sveltekit()] });
```

It turns barrel imports into leaf imports at transform time. A barrel is a module that only re-exports other modules (an `index.ts`, a package's `components.ts`, an `export * from` fan-out). Importing one name from it drags in the whole graph: in dev every module behind it is transformed and served, in a build the chunk graph follows the import graph before tree-shaking, and a package that is not `sideEffects: false` keeps all of it. With `barrels()` listed, `import { Button } from '$lib/components'` is rewritten to `import Button from '…/components/Button.svelte'` before Svelte or ogygia's region transform see the file, so the graph contains only the leaves the file actually uses.

```ts
barrels()                                        // every pure barrel in the project
barrels({ packages: ['@acme/ui'] })              // dependencies too, by name / glob / regex
barrels({ force: ['$lib/registry'] })            // an impure barrel: leaves moved, `import 'barrel'` kept
barrels({ keep: [/stores\/index\.ts$/] })        // never touch these
```

What it does and does not do:

- **Project modules need no listing.** Every file outside `node_modules` is a candidate, and purity decides. A barrel is _pure_ when every top-level statement is an import, a re-export, a type or `export {}`. A module with code of its own next to its re-exports is left alone unless it is listed in `force`, in which case its re-exported names move and a bare `import 'barrel'` is kept so that its own code still runs.
- **Dependencies are opt-in** through `packages`, because Vite pre-bundles bare imports in dev, and a leaf import that skips the optimizer while another importer gets the pre-bundled copy produces two instances of one module. List a package when you know it is safe (for example an ESM component library that is excluded from `optimizeDeps`).
- **Nested barrels, `export *` fan-outs, rename chains and `import … ; export { … }` bindings** are followed to the real leaf. A name that two `export *` statements disagree on, a name the barrel does not export, and a name behind something that cannot be parsed (JSON, CommonJS, a `?raw` import) stay on the barrel import.
- **Import attributes and `type` modifiers are carried over** (`with { type: 'json' }`, `import { type T }`), and sourcemaps are emitted.
- **Regions are left untouched.** An import that carries region marks (`with { wake }`, `render`, `preset`, `region`, or the app's `importKeys`) is never rewritten, default or named, so a marked import means exactly what it meant before: the region is keyed on that import.
- `import * as ns from 'barrel'`, `import 'barrel'` and dynamic imports are left as written.

Options: `packages`, `force`, `keep` (matchers: a package name or specifier prefix, a `*` glob, a regex, or a function of the resolved id), `importers: { include, exclude }` (which files are rewritten; default project files), `followPackages` (walk `export *` into dependencies while mapping a barrel; default `true`), `report` (the end-of-build report: `true` = the eight biggest barrels and a count of the rest, `'all'` = every barrel, `false` = silent), `debug` (log every rewrite).

### `presets`

Presets are named bundles of strategy and options that an import references with `preset: 'name'`. A preset uses the same grammar as an inline import, namely `render` (the mode), `wake` (an island's schedule) or `fetch` (a hole's schedule), plus the tuning options that are not allowed inline (`margin`, `maxAge`, …):

```ts
ogygia({
  presets: {
    chart: { render: 'static', wake: 'visible', margin: '200px' },
    pricing: { render: 'deferred', fetch: 'load', maxAge: '1h' },
    ticker: { render: 'live', wake: 'idle', maxAge: '10m' }
  }
})
```

```svelte
<script>
  import Chart from './Chart.svelte' with { preset: 'chart' };
</script>
```

Fields: `render` (`'static'` | `'deferred'` | `'live'`), `wake` (an island's schedule), `fetch` (a hole's schedule), `margin`, `maxAge`, `onExpire`, `revalidate`, `keep`, `prefetch`.

#### `prefetch`: warm a `deferred` hole before it swaps

A hole's `fetch` marks a single moment, when its HTML is fetched **and** swapped in, and `prefetch` separates the two. The HTML is warmed into the frame store on the `prefetch` schedule (`'load'` | `'idle'` | `'visible'` | a media query), while the swap still waits for `fetch`. An on-demand hole (`fetch: 'interaction'`) therefore already has its bytes before the first hover, and the gesture uses the warm frame instead of paying for a round trip to the origin:

```ts
ogygia({ presets: { menu: { render: 'deferred', fetch: 'interaction', prefetch: 'idle', maxAge: '1h' } } })
```

```svelte
<script>
  import CountryPanel from './CountryPanel.svelte' with { render: 'deferred', fetch: 'interaction', prefetch: 'idle' };
</script>
```

`prefetch` can be written inline or in a preset. It applies only to `deferred` holes, since an island's JS is preloaded by the runtime when it wakes rather than by this option. It is never `'interaction'`, because that is the swap and therefore `fetch`'s job, and it is never the hole's own `fetch` schedule, which would add nothing. Without `prefetch`, an `interaction` hole keeps its default behaviour, where the first hover fetches and swaps in one step.

#### `maxAge`: caching a `deferred` hole

A `render: 'deferred'` hole is **dynamic by default**: its endpoint is served with `Cache-Control: no-store`, so it renders again on every request (for a per-request clock or a personalized figure, for example). `maxAge` opts a hole into a browser cache instead, given as seconds (a number) or as a duration string (`'30s'` | `'5m'` | `'1h'`):

```ts
ogygia({ presets: { pricing: { render: 'deferred', fetch: 'load', maxAge: '1h' } } })
```

That hole's endpoint is then served with `Cache-Control: private, max-age=3600`. The `maxAge` is **signed into the hole's URL**, so a harvested capability cannot be redirected to a longer cache. `maxAge: 0`, or leaving it out, keeps the hole dynamic. With `render: 'live'`, `maxAge` instead sets how stale the client lets the region get before revalidating.

::: warning A bare number changes units by context
On a **deferred hole**, `maxAge: 60` means 60 **seconds**, because it becomes an HTTP `max-age`, which is measured in seconds. On a **live / remount lake**, `maxAge: 60` means 60 **milliseconds**, because it is client-side staleness and is measured in ms like every JS timer. The same key therefore uses two different clocks. Duration strings (`'30s'`, `'5m'`, `'1h'`) mean the same thing everywhere, so prefer them and the problem goes away.
:::

### `importKeys`

Extra import-attribute keys to treat as ogygia directives, for interoperability with other tooling that reads import attributes.

### `rateLimit`

Protects the signed island endpoint against abuse by limiting requests per window per client.

The client is the address your adapter gives (`event.getClientAddress()`). Behind a reverse proxy or a load balancer, tell the adapter which header holds the visitor's address (for adapter-node, `ADDRESS_HEADER`, with `XFF_DEPTH` for `X-Forwarded-For`). Otherwise every request has the proxy's address, every visitor shares one budget, and normal traffic gets `429`. When the budget runs out for a private, loopback or link-local address, the server warns once with [`rate_limit_shared_address`](https://ogygiajs.dev/e/rate_limit_shared_address).

### `sessionCookie`

The name and options of the session cookie that ogygia uses to scope signed capabilities.

### `ttl`

How long a signed region capability URL stays valid, in seconds (default `3600`, clamped to 60–86400). Keep it short, so that a harvested URL expires.

Holes outlive it where they need to. Prerendered and postrendered pages sign their holes for the life of the deploy. On a page whose load marks it as shared-cacheable, for example with `setHeaders({ 'cache-control': 'public, s-maxage=604800' })`, an anonymous hole is signed for the page's cache lifetime. A hole on an older cached copy renews itself when it wakes, because the endpoint re-signs a URL it issued (for anonymous holes only) for up to [`maxPageAge`](#ogygiavite-maxpageage) after it expired. A `cache-control` header that a handle sets after the render is invisible when the URL is signed, so set it from a load.

### `maxPageAge`

The maximum age, in seconds, at which a cached copy of a page can still get its holes (default 8 days). It sets how long after a hole's URL expired the hole can still renew itself. Beyond that, the hole keeps its fallback and its request is answered with `403` and `x-ogygia-deny: renew-window`. `0` turns renewal off.

Raise it when your CDN keeps pages for longer than 8 days and the page does not say so in its `cache-control` (because the TTL is set in the CDN's own configuration):

```ts
ogygia({ maxPageAge: 30 * 24 * 3600 });
```

It is not the same as a longer `ttl`. A renewed URL still lives only for `ttl`, and a hole bound to a session never renews. A renewable URL replays the props signed into it, so limit a hole's props to routing keys (a locale, a page) and read anything sensitive at render time.

### `OGYGIA_SECRET`

The HMAC secret that signs `render: 'deferred'` capability URLs. Set it in the environment to keep signatures stable across a deployment; otherwise a key is generated per build. Every deployment that serves your pages or your islands endpoint needs the same value at runtime, because a hole signed with one key and checked against another is refused (`x-ogygia-deny: foreign-key`).

If your server process never sees its runtime environment, pass the secret to the server handle instead. A value from `$env/static/private` is read at build time and built into the server:

```ts
// src/hooks.server.ts
import { OGYGIA_SECRET } from '$env/static/private';
import * as ogygia from 'ogygia/server';

export const handle = ogygia.handle({ secret: OGYGIA_SECRET });
```

`handle({ secret })` takes precedence over the environment variable, and an empty value counts as not given. The same secret signs the same way through either path, so pages signed with one verify with the other. Like the environment variable, it needs at least 16 bytes in production.

The server decides its key the first time it signs or checks a capability, and keeps it for the life of the process. A different secret arriving after that (from a second `handle()`, or after a render) is an error: it throws in dev and is logged in production, and the first key stays.

A hole's URL signs the component it renders, so a page cached across a deploy keeps its holes as long as their components still exist, regardless of a changed preset, `wake` or build-time `OGYGIA_SECRET`. A hole whose component was moved, renamed or removed is refused (`x-ogygia-deny: unknown-id`) and keeps its fallback.

Server islands and `render: 'live'` need the [server handle](/docs/start/install) installed to serve the endpoint.

## `ogygia/server`

```ts
import * as ogygia from 'ogygia/server';
export const handle = ogygia.handle();
```

`handle(options)` is the SvelteKit server handle. It serves the signed region endpoint (per-hole loads and the single-flight navigation batch) and injects client seeds.

| Option     | What it does                                                                                                                    |
| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `endpoint` | The region endpoint's path. Default `/__ogygia__`.                                                                              |
| `secret`   | The secret that signs capability URLs. Takes precedence over `OGYGIA_SECRET`; see [`OGYGIA_SECRET`](#ogygiavite-ogygia-secret). |

An app's own pass over the HTML (a web-component server render) is not an option here: it is `reshape.handle(transform)` from the `@ogygia/reshape` package. See [Server transforms](/docs/app/server-transforms).

## `@ogygia/reshape`

```ts
import { handle, scanRegions, liftRegions, restoreRegions, transformMarkup, restore } from '@ogygia/reshape';
import type { ReshapeTransform } from '@ogygia/reshape';
```

A package of its own (`pnpm add @ogygia/reshape`); it replaces `ogygia/markup`, which is gone. Its `reshape()` Vite plugin comes from `@ogygia/reshape/vite`. The markup helpers need no Kit, so they run in the non-SvelteKit half of a monorepo or in a test.

| Export                                                 | What it is                                                                                                               |
| ------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `handle(transform)`                                    | The app's own pass, as a Kit handle before `ogygia.handle()`. See [Server transforms](/docs/app/server-transforms).      |
| `ReshapeTransform`                                     | The type of the function `handle()` takes, for a transform written in a module of its own.                               |
| `scanRegions(html)`                                    | Yields every region in a document (island, lake or hole), with its span and attributes.                                  |
| `liftRegions(html)` · `restoreRegions(shell, regions)` | Lift the regions out around a foreign SSR pass, and splice them back with the marks the pass stamped.                    |
| `transformMarkup(html, transform, { kind, csr, dev })` | The server round trip of `handle(transform)`: mark, your transform, settle.                                              |
| `checkScript(check)`                                   | The dev check's payload (`settled.check` with `dev: true`), to put in front of the markup so `restore` checks each host. |
| `localizeMarks(block)`                                 | Renumbers a block's marks from 0 for a render cache key; `.back(rendered)` puts the document's ids back.                 |
| `restore(root)`                                        | The browser half, on a parsed document or fragment (jsdom included).                                                     |

## `ogygia/app`

Replacements for `$app/navigation` that are safe to use in islands, because a csr=false page has no Kit client:

`goto` · `invalidate` · `invalidateAll` · `preloadData` · `preloadCode` · `beforeNavigate` · `afterNavigate` · `disableScrollHandling`

Use these instead of `$app/navigation` inside islands. See [SPA router](/docs/app/spa-router).

## `ogygia/events`

`ogygia/events` is the event layer: the typed stream of what the framework did (island wakes, server renders, navigations), plus the bus and sink helpers (`add_sink`, `snapshot`, `clear`). It is compiled in only when a plugin asks for it, as the devtools plugin does. The analysis engine shared by the dock, the profiler and the test helpers is its own package, `@ogygia/insights` (there is no `ogygia/insights`): install it and import from it.

## Plugin packages

Each feature below is a separate package that you install and list once beside `ogygia()` in your Vite config, as described in [Packages](/docs/start/packages). Each has its own section in these docs, with its own entry points and API pages.

| Package                                           | Plugin                           | Imports in app code                                                                                                                                            |
| ------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| [`@ogygia/content`](/docs/content/overview)       | `content({ markdown, presets })` | `@ogygia/content`, `/server`, `/markdown`, `/docs-shell`, `/slot`, `/theme.css`, `/shell.css`, `/types`; see [Entry points & config](/docs/content/api/config) |
| [`@ogygia/flags`](/docs/flags/overview)           | `flags()`                        | `flag`, `decide` from `@ogygia/flags`; `openfeature`, `ofrep` from `@ogygia/flags/openfeature`                                                                 |
| [`@ogygia/postrender`](/docs/postrender/overview) | `postrender({ ttl, default })`   | `postrender` and the store and CDN adapters from `@ogygia/postrender`                                                                                          |
| [`@ogygia/federation`](/docs/federation/overview) | `federation()`                   | `federate`, `mount` from `@ogygia/federation`                                                                                                                  |
| [`@ogygia/router`](/docs/router/overview)         | `router()`                       | `routes`, `page`, … from `@ogygia/router`; `api` from `@ogygia/router/api`                                                                                     |
| [`@ogygia/devtools`](/docs/devtools/overview)     | `devtools({ dock, profiler })`   | `@ogygia/devtools` (event sinks), `/report`, `/profiler`, `/profiler/client`, `/profiler/storage/*`                                                            |
| [`@ogygia/testing`](/docs/testing/overview)       | none (a test helper)             | `@ogygia/testing`                                                                                                                                              |
| [`@ogygia/cli`](/docs/cli/overview)               | none (the command)               | `npx ogygia …`; see [Commands](/docs/cli/commands)                                                                                                             |
| [`@ogygia/core`](/docs/core/overview)             | none (for plugin authors)        | `@ogygia/core/plugin`, `/plugin/client`, `/plugin/utils`, …; see [Writing a plugin](/docs/core/plugins)                                                        |

`@ogygia/tsdown-plugin-svelte` is not in the table, because nothing in an app imports it: it is a tsdown plugin for building component libraries. Its [section](/docs/tsdown-plugin-svelte/overview) has its options and API.
